    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d173">

                <pb facs="0353" n="235" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg235"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/10033: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-06-05T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 5, 1928—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 5—10:40
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>426. 1. Upon the departure last night of Chang Hsueh-liang,<anchor n="68"
                        xml:id="d173-f1"/> Yang Yu-ting<anchor n="69" xml:id="d173-f2"/> and the
                    principal remaining officials connected with the Fengtien regime, the control of
                    Peking is in the hands of the committee of elder statesmen headed by Wang
                        Shih-tseng,<anchor n="70" xml:id="d173-f3"/> mentioned <pb facs="0354"
                        n="236" xml:id="pg236"/> in paragraph 4 of my number 409, May 31, 10 p.m.
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">a.m.</hi>].<anchor n="71" xml:id="d173-f4"/> One
                    reliable Fengtien brigade of about five thousand men remains to preserve order
                    in the city. Plans are under negotiation for the peaceful withdrawal of this
                    force upon the arrival of the incoming Nationalist troops. Forces of Yen<anchor
                        n="72" xml:id="d173-f5"/> and Feng<anchor n="73" xml:id="d173-f6"/> are
                    reported within day’s march of city and question of which will first arrive is
                    purely speculative.</p>
                <p>2. All Manchurian troops are clearing this area without disorder. Heavy troop
                    traffic has prevented operation of passenger trains between here and Tientsin
                    since June 3rd. Both Tientsin and Peking remain quiet and there are apparent[ly]
                    no signs of agitation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d173-f1" n="68" xml:id="d173-f1-content">
                <p>Son of Chang Tso-lin, and commander of Fengtien Third Army, under the Peking
                    Government.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d173-f2" n="69" xml:id="d173-f2-content">
                <p>Chief of Staff in Chang Tso-lin’s forces.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d173-f3" n="70" xml:id="d173-f3-content">
                <p>Refers apparently to Gen. Wang Chih-chen, chairman of the Committee of
                    Safety.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d173-f4" n="71" xml:id="d173-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d173-f5" n="72" xml:id="d173-f5-content">
                <p>Marshal Yen Hsi-shan.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d173-f6" n="73" xml:id="d173-f6-content">
                <p>Marshal Feng Yu-hsiang.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
